description:
Location: src/filenode.rs:108-117.

    /// Collision-handling policy for FileNode/set create operations.
    #[derive(Debug)]
    enum OnExists {
        /// null / absent (default) — return alreadyExists.
        Reject,
        /// 'replace' — destroy the existing node and create the new one.
        Replace,
        /// 'rename' — find a non-colliding name by appending a counter suffix.
        Rename,
    }

What idiomatic Rust would do for a unit-variant control enum:

    #[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]
    enum OnExists { Reject, Replace, Rename }

For unit-variant enums (no payload), Copy is free (the variant is a single discriminant byte). Clone follows from Copy. PartialEq + Eq are free-derive and document 'this is a value type'. Hash unlocks use in HashSet/HashMap if a future caller wants it.

What goes wrong as-is — three things, none catastrophic but real:

1. **Move-out-of-shared analysis fragility.** The current code does 'match on_exists { OnExists::Reject => ..., OnExists::Replace => ..., OnExists::Rename => ... }' INSIDE a 'for (create_id, obj_val) in create_map' loop (filenode.rs:237). This works today because the unit-variant discriminant read does not move the value, but the shape is non-obvious. If a future contributor changes any arm to bind a future payload (e.g. OnExists::Rename(usize) for the suffix-max), the for-loop body stops compiling on the second iteration. Deriving Copy makes the value semantics explicit and the move analysis trivial.

2. **Testability.** Without PartialEq, the enum cannot be asserted on in tests (assert_eq!(on_exists, OnExists::Reject) fails to compile). Today no test does this, but the absence of PartialEq is a signal that the author treated the enum as opaque rather than as data.

3. **Documentation by derive.** The standard derive set documents the type's role. A unit-variant enum with only Debug reads as 'placeholder, may change'. The standard derives say 'this is a value type with the obvious semantics'. Self-documenting code matters at the public-vs-private API boundary, but it also matters for crate-internal types when contributors are cycling through unfamiliar files.

The current code happens to work, so this is a maintainability / clarity finding, not a correctness one. Filed at P2 (clippy-ish) under idiomatic-rust,type-design.

Canonical comparison: jmap-mail-server does not have an analogous OnExists enum (FileNode-specific feature). The mail-server's similar local control enums (e.g. AllowedAttributes type in submission.rs) derive the standard set.
